@@ -104,6 +104,18 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (exactly-once). Deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) is implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` with the three call-panel modes below (`any` applies fairness; `priority_only`/`normal_only` ignore it). Both call paths enforce the **same effective queue authorization rule** (`QueuePolicy::call`): owner/admin bypass without a grant row, employees require a `queue_access.call` grant for that queue, non-members are denied with no ticket mutation. HTTP/Livewire panels remain future work.
 
+### Display code
+
+The code shown on the totem and TV is **one uppercase letter followed by at least three digits** (`N001`, `P002`, `E001`, … `N1000`). The letter tells the priority level apart at a glance.
+
+- **Letter by priority:** default `0` → `N` (Normal), `10` → `P` (Priority), `20` → `E` (80+). Values not in that map fall back by range: `<= 0` → `N`, `>= 20` → `E`, anything in between → `P`.
+- **Per-queue map:** a queue may override the letter for any priority value (`queues.priority_prefix_map`, e.g. `{"0": "X", "10": "Y", "20": "Z"}`). A mapped value wins; unmapped values use the default. An empty map means defaults. The map is changed only through `App\Actions\UpdateQueue` (owner/admin) and is validated on every save:
+  - keys are canonical non-negative integers (`"foo"`, `"-1"`, `"010"` are invalid);
+  - values are exactly one uppercase letter `A`–`Z`;
+  - effective letters are unique: the custom entries plus the default for each of the 0/10/20 levels the map does not cover (so `{"0": "N", "10": "N"}` and `{"0": "E"}`, which would merge Normal with 80+, are rejected).
+- **Daily sequence:** numbering restarts every day, per queue and per letter. The next code is the last valid code with the same letter issued the same day, plus one; the first of the day is `<letter>001`. Malformed codes are skipped, legacy `A…` codes do not belong to any letter's sequence, and numbers freed by deleted tickets are not reused.
+- **One clock read:** `App\Actions\IssueTicket` reads the clock once and uses that instant for the code's day, `issued_at` and `issue_date`, so a ticket issued at 23:59:59 stays on that day's sequence.
+
 ### Priority
 
 Priority is an **integer**: the **higher the value, the more prioritized**. Named levels are configurable presets per entity that map to values; ordering uses the number, not the label.
